Amazon EFS — Theory for AWS Cloud/DevOps Fresher

Amazon Web Services EFS (Elastic File System) is a managed, scalable file storage service in AWS that allows multiple servers, such as EC2 instances, to access the same files simultaneously over the network.

1. What problem does EFS solve?

Suppose you have multiple EC2 instances running an application:

          ┌── EC2-1 ──┐
          │            │
Users → Load Balancer → EC2-2
          │            │
          └── EC2-3 ──┘
                 │
                 ▼
              Amazon EFS
             Shared Files

If files are stored only on one EC2 instance, other instances may not have access to them.

EFS provides shared storage, so all EC2 instances can access the same files.

2. Key characteristics
Feature	EFS
Storage type	File storage
Access protocol	NFS
Scalability	Automatically grows/shrinks
Multiple EC2 access	Yes
Managed by AWS	Yes
Server management	Not required
Typical use	Shared application files
Availability	Regional
Linux support	Yes
3. How EFS works

The basic flow is:

EC2 Instance
     │
     │ NFS
     ▼
EFS Mount Target
     │
     ▼
EFS File System

An EC2 instance mounts EFS like a normal filesystem.

After mounting, the application can work with files using normal Linux commands:

ls
cd
mkdir
cp
rm

The important difference is that the data is stored in EFS, not on the EC2 instance's local filesystem.

4. EFS vs EBS

This is a very common interview question.

EFS	EBS
File storage	Block storage
Can be shared by multiple EC2 instances	Usually attached to one EC2 at a time
Uses NFS	Attached as a block device
Automatically scales	Provisioned volume size
Regional	AZ-specific
Good for shared files	Good for OS/application/database disks
Simple example

EBS:

EC2 → EBS

EFS:

EC2-1 ─┐
EC2-2 ─┼──→ EFS
EC2-3 ─┘
5. EFS vs S3

Another important interview comparison:

EFS	S3
File storage	Object storage
Mounted as filesystem	Accessed through API/SDK/HTTP
Uses NFS	Uses S3 API
Designed for file-based workloads	Designed for objects/files
Good for shared application filesystem	Good for backups, images, logs, static files

For example:

Shared WordPress files → EFS
Website images/backups → S3
6. EFS Components
File System

The main EFS storage resource where your files are stored.

Mount Target

A network endpoint through which an EC2 instance accesses EFS.

EC2
 │
 ▼
Mount Target
 │
 ▼
EFS

Mount targets are associated with Availability Zones/subnets.

Access Point

An application-specific entry point into EFS.

It can help control:

Root directory
POSIX user/group
File permissions

Useful when different applications need controlled access to the same EFS filesystem.

7. EFS Storage Classes

EFS provides different storage classes based on access frequency.

EFS Standard

For frequently accessed files.

EFS Infrequent Access — EFS IA

For files that are accessed less frequently and can help reduce storage cost.

EFS Archive

For very rarely accessed files where lower storage cost is important.

EFS can use Lifecycle Management to automatically move files between storage classes based on access patterns.

8. EFS Performance Modes

Two important performance modes:

General Purpose

Designed for most applications and is the normal choice for many workloads.

Max I/O

Designed for workloads requiring very high levels of parallelism, with a trade-off in latency.

For a fresher interview, remember:

General Purpose = common workloads
Max I/O = highly parallel workloads

9. Throughput Modes

EFS supports different ways of providing throughput, including:

Elastic
Provisioned
Bursting
Elastic

Throughput automatically scales based on workload.

Provisioned

You specify the throughput you need independently of the amount of data stored.

Bursting

Throughput can increase temporarily based on the filesystem's storage and workload characteristics.

10. Security

EFS security is important because EC2 accesses it through the network.

Main things involved:

Security Groups

Control network traffic to the EFS mount target.

For NFS, the important port is:

TCP 2049

Typical flow:

EC2 Security Group
       │
       │ TCP 2049
       ▼
EFS Mount Target Security Group
IAM

Can be used with EFS authorization mechanisms, particularly with EFS access points and IAM identity-based authorization.

POSIX permissions

Linux file permissions also control access:

owner
group
others

So EFS access can involve both network-level security and file-level permissions.

11. Availability and Durability

EFS is designed as a regional file system.

It can provide access across multiple Availability Zones, which makes it useful for highly available applications.

Example:

          Load Balancer
          /     |     \
        EC2    EC2    EC2
         \      |      /
          \     |     /
             EFS

If one EC2 instance fails, another EC2 instance can continue accessing the shared files.

12. Common Real-World Uses

EFS is commonly useful for:

Shared application files
Web servers
WordPress shared files
Content management systems
Container workloads
Shared configuration/data
Home directories
Applications running across multiple EC2 instances
Example: WordPress

Suppose:

EC2-1 → WordPress
EC2-2 → WordPress
EC2-3 → WordPress

All three instances may need access to the same uploaded media.

Instead of keeping those files separately on each EC2:

EC2-1 ─┐
EC2-2 ─┼──→ EFS → WordPress files
EC2-3 ─┘

Now all instances can access the shared files.